BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:privacy-camp-2026-call-for-sessions@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260731T085604Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Privacy Camp 2026 (EDRi): Call for Sessions\, The Origins
  of Future Technologies
DESCRIPTION:Privacy Camp holds its 14th edition on 13 October 2026 in Bruss
 els and online\, organised by European Digital Rights (EDRi) with the Rese
 arch Group on Law\, Science\, Technology & Society (LSTS) at Vrije Univers
 iteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies at 
 Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center. 
 THEME: 'The Origins of Future Technologies: Towards communities of digital
  self-determination'. The call starts from the premise that digital future
 s do not appear from nowhere but are built through land\, labour\, capital
 \, infrastructure\, law\, public money\, political choices and social stru
 ggle\, and asks where technologies come from\, who gets to shape them\, an
 d what conditions make digital self-determination possible. It situates th
 is against the EU's 2026 Tech Sovereignty Package (semiconductors\, artifi
 cial intelligence\, cloud infrastructure and data centres) and the wider d
 eregulatory agenda including the Digital Omnibus\, the AI Omnibus and refo
 rms affecting data protection\, privacy\, labour and environmental safegua
 rds\, asking whose version of sovereignty is actually being discussed. TOP
 IC AREAS: (1) Material conditions in the production of technologies\, cove
 ring the natural resources needed across the supply chain (critical compon
 ents such as semiconductors\, water for data centres) and how GDPR\, ePriv
 acy and AI Act safeguards shape the material conditions of AI from data ex
 traction and biometric processing through to data centres\, labour and pub
 lic infrastructure\; labour conditions\, including who mines critical mate
 rials\, union-busting across the tech supply chain\, and coalitions betwee
 n tech workers\, unions\, migrant justice groups\, climate movements and d
 igital rights organisations\; and capital\, meaning the venture capital fo
 rces driving the current AI hype and consolidating through defence\, secur
 ity and militarisation. (2) Political vision in the use of technologies\, 
 covering military use and how militarisation and securitisation shape the 
 EU deregulation debate\, and the political ideologies driving technology d
 evelopment\, including what digital self-determination means for communiti
 es facing racialised discrimination\, surveillance\, occupation\, border v
 iolence\, workplace control or environmental harm. (3) Moving technologies
  forward through communities\, covering community innovation for digital s
 elf-determination (distributed and self-hosted data storage\, AI built by 
 and for community needs\, community-led\, public or cooperative digital in
 frastructure)\; law\, deregulation and rights (resisting the Digital Omnib
 us and specifically the Data Omnibus\, defending GDPR and ePrivacy safegua
 rds\, protecting access\, transparency and automated decision-making right
 s\, enforcing existing digital laws\, supporting collective litigation\, a
 nd challenging the framing of rights as obstacles to EU competitiveness)\;
  and building power by connecting grassroots movements across tech worker 
 organising\, labour\, migrant\, LGBTQIA+\, climate\, economic justice and 
 landback movements. FORMATS: interactive workshops are particularly encour
 aged\, meaning small-group work\, peer exchange and collaboration across s
 ectors\, coalitions and disciplines\, moving from talk to strategic action
 \; panel discussions are also welcome\, especially those centring diverse 
 voices and grounded lived experience with room for meaningful audience eng
 agement. SUBMISSION GUIDELINES: state a clear objective for the session an
 d what a good outcome would be\; make it as interactive as possible\; supp
 ort a diversity of voices and multiple perspectives\; average session leng
 th is 50 minutes\; propose the format that actually suits the idea rather 
 than forcing a panel into a workshop or vice versa. For workshops\, make t
 he objectives\, structure and facilitation clear and have participants wor
 k together in small groups towards a specific goal\; the organisers provid
 e additional guidance\, support and feedback for workshops. For panels\, l
 ist a maximum of 4 speakers covering academia\, civil society and decision
 -maker perspectives\, and indicate which speakers have confirmed at least 
 in principle. TIMELINE: proposals due via the online form by 10 August 202
 6 at 09:00 CEST\; the Content Committee notifies applicants before 21 Augu
 st 2026\; confirmation to proceed required by 25 August\; draft programme 
 published 4 September\; speakers and final session description confirmed b
 y 10 September at the latest (earlier confirmation improves the odds that 
 the EUR 400 reimbursement budget covers speaker travel and accommodation)\
 ; final programme published 28 September. The 2026 Content Committee is An
 dreea Belu (EDRi)\, Gloria González Fuster (LSTS\, VUB)\, Jill Toh (Racis
 m and Technology Center)\, Sanne Stevens (Justice\, Equity and Technology 
 Project) and Alexandra Giannopoulou (DFF). Programme enquiries: andreea.be
 lu@edri.org. Venue and reimbursement enquiries: guillermo.peris@edri.org.\
 n\nOrganisation: European Digital Rights (EDRi)\, with LSTS at Vrije Unive
 rsiteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies a
 t Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center
 \nLocation: Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 
 2026.\nAward: NO speaker fee or honorarium. Where needed\, up to EUR 400 p
 er speaker or session organiser towards transport to and accommodation in 
 Brussels\, reimbursed against invoices and travel tickets\; those with org
 anisational support are encouraged to cover their own expenses. No budget 
 beyond this cap. Free to propose and free to attend (registration open).\n
 Duration: One-day conference on 13 October 2026. Average session length 50
  minutes.\n\nMore: https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 2026
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultuurloket-digitall-implementatiebijdrage-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260731T085604Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultuurloket DigitALL: Implementation Contribution (Imple
 mentatiebijdrage\, 2026)
DESCRIPTION:A one-off 2026 call letting Dutch cultural institutions reuse a
 nd adapt digital technology that other institutions have already built and
  published in the open DigitALL Toolkit\, for example translation software
  shared between theatres or a streaming platform adapted for a new venue. 
 Grants run up to EUR 30\,000 and cover technical modification\, integratio
 n\, production and deployment costs. Eligible applicants are museums\, the
 atres\, music venues\, festivals\, film theatres and professional performi
 ng arts producers. Applications are submitted online and assessed in order
  of receipt rather than comparatively\, with a decision within ten weeks. 
 The deadline was extended from 31 May 2026 to 1 November 2026 after high d
 emand. IMPORTANT RESTRICTION: this route cannot be used to further develop
  your own pilot internally\; it exists specifically to spread another inst
 itution's working technology across the sector. For developing something n
 ew\, use the DigitALL pilot contribution instead.\n\nOrganisation: Cultuur
 loket DigitALL\nLocation: Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingd
 om. No city or region restriction.\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000. Covers techn
 ical modification\, system integration\, production and deployment costs. 
 Hardware purchase alone is ineligible unless clearly essential to the publ
 ic experience. No stated co-financing percentage. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Not specified\; scoped to a single implementation.\n\nMore: https:/
 /cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingdom. No city or reg
 ion restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eic-accelerator-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260731T085604Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260708
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260709
SUMMARY:Deadline: EIC Accelerator 2026 (European Innovation Council)
DESCRIPTION:The EIC Accelerator funds high-risk\, high-impact deep-tech SME
 s and start-ups to develop and scale breakthrough innovations. It offers a
  grant of up to EUR 2.5 million (a non-dilutive lump sum) for innovation a
 ctivities\, and can combine this with an equity investment of EUR 1-10 mil
 lion through the EIC Fund (blended finance)\, or provide investment only. 
 The Step 1 short application can be submitted at any time\; full (Step 2) 
 applications are evaluated at fixed cut-offs. The next 2026 cut-offs are 8
  July\, 2 September and 4 November 2026 (all 17:00 Brussels time). Eligibl
 e applicants are for-profit SMEs and start-ups (fewer than 250 employees\,
  turnover up to EUR 50M or balance sheet up to EUR 43M) established in an 
 EU Member State or Horizon Europe Associated Country\; small mid-caps up t
 o 499 employees are eligible for investment-only support. Apply via the EU
  Funding and Tenders portal (topic HORIZON-EIC-2026-ACCELERATOR-01).\n\nOr
 ganisation: European Innovation Council (European Commission)\nLocation: E
 U Member States and Horizon Europe Associated Countries. Single-company ap
 plicants\; third-country applicants must relocate before the full-applicat
 ion stage.\nAward: Grant up to EUR 2.5 million (non-dilutive lump sum) for
  innovation activities\, plus optional equity investment of EUR 1-10 milli
 on via the EIC Fund. Options: grant-only\, blended finance\, or investment
 -only. No application fee.\nDuration: Project-based\; supports development
  and scale-up at TRL 5/6 to 8.\n\nMore: https://eic.ec.europa.eu/eic-fundi
 ng-opportunities/eic-accelerator_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
URL:https://eic.ec.europa.eu/eic-funding-opportunities/eic-accelerator_en?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:EU Member States and Horizon Europe Associated Countries. Single-c
 ompany applicants\; third-country applicants must relocate before the full
 -application stage.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stimuleringsfonds-digital-culture-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260731T085604Z
